# Known, accepted drift. Format:
#   <slug>|<substring of the drift message>   # why, and what the fix is
#
# Narrower than .lint-skip, which exempts a tool from every check. An entry
# here keeps the tool checked for everything else, so --strict can gate on new
# drift while this backlog is worked through.
#
# A waiver is a promise to come back, not a fix. Deleting a line should be the
# last step of fixing the thing it describes.

# ── Club colour sources (opened 30/07/2026) ──────────────────────────────────
# Four of the original six were closed on 15/08/2026: fixtures-results,
# league-tables, single-fixture and top-scorers now read the club list through
# NL.clubs.all(). Their waivers said they fetched raw.githubusercontent, which
# had stopped being true — somebody moved them to a same-origin path and left
# the waiver behind. A waiver that describes a fixed problem is worse than no
# waiver: it makes the backlog look longer than it is and the entry unreadable.
#
# One is left, and it is the one that matters. clubs-data.js has 29 of 72
# primaries drifted from clubs-meta, so closing it CHANGES WHAT THE TOOL DRAWS.
# That needs a before/after look by someone who knows what the graphic should
# be, not a find-and-replace.
graphics/table-graphic|stale clubs-data.js mirror        # 29 primaries wrong; needs a look before/after
